------------------------------------------------ Router ------------------------------------------------

- HAVE NICE URLS THAT MIRROR THE CONTENT DISPLAYED USING THE ROUTER.

- Instruction: Angular Prerequisite "Router" chapter

1️⃣ Until now, we have added our components directly inside the main component. Now, we would like to
    create a new page in the application. We want to view the Races at the URL /races, and we want the
    home page to display another component Home.
    Modify the template app.html so that it only contains the menu and the routerOutlet:

        <pr-menu />
        <main class="container" style="margin-top: 70px">
            <router-outlet />
        </main>


2️⃣ First, we need to build the router configuration. Create a file app.routes.ts inside src/app. It
    will contain a constant of type Routes, named routes, representing an array of routes. Export that
    constant. Add a route in that array that maps the URL /races to the component Races.


3️⃣ We then just have to use this constant inside the app.config.ts file: add provideRouter(routes) to
    the providers and all the configuration is done.

        export const appConfig: ApplicationConfig = {
            providers: [provideBrowserGlobalErrorListeners(), provideRouter(routes)]
        };


4️⃣ By the way, we should create a component for the home page of the application. This component would
    display the title, and the link to navigate to the races.
    Let’s do that! Create a component Home  with Angular CLI.
    For the template, use:

    home.html:
        <div class="text-center" style="margin-top: 100px">
            <h1>Ponyracer <small>Always a pleasure to bet on ponies</small></h1>
        </div>

        <div class="text-center">
            <!-- insert the link with the routerLink here -->
        </div>
    
    and complete it with a link <a class="btn btn-primary" routerLink="??">Races</a>, allowing to
    navigate to Races, using routerLink to generate the link (don’t forget to add RouterLink to the
    imports of the component).


5️⃣ Finally, configure the routes in app.routes.ts to add a route mapping the URL '/' to the component
    Home.


6️⃣ We also would like our menu to be more functional.
    Thanks to routerLink, make sure that a click on Ponyracer goes to the home page, and that a click on
    Races goes to the list of races. You can also remove the field title from app.ts which is not used.


7️⃣ Do not forget to copy all the test files and run ng test to be sure that everything is OK.